research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-249-
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-250-**Given:** A sequence $(f^{(n)})_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ in $K$ that is Cauchy in $K$.
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-251-
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-252-[L1] $K$ consists of the functions $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{R}$ whose support is bounded below; $t^{-a}$ is $1$ at index $a$ and $0$ elsewhere ([[def-formal-laurent-series]]).
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-253-
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md:254:[L2] $K$ is an ordered field, so its order is transitive and total ([[thm-laurent-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]], [[def-abs-value]]); and $(g - h)(j) = g(j) - h(j)$ for $g, h \in K$ ([[lem-laurent-series-ring]]).
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-255-
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-256-[L3] In $K$: $0_K < t^{-(k+1)} < t^{-k}$ for every $k \in \mathbb{Z}$; for every $\varepsilon > 0$ in $K$ there is $k \in \mathbb{N}$ with $t^{-k} < \varepsilon$; if $h(j) = 0$ for every $j \le k$ then $|h| < t^{-k}$; and if $|h| < t^{-k}$ then $h(j) = 0$ for every $j < k$ ([[lem-laurent-non-archimedean]]).
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-257-
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-258-[L4] $(x_n)$ is Cauchy in $K$ when for every $\varepsilon > 0$ in $K$ there is $N \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|x_n - x_m| < \varepsilon$ for all $n, m \ge N$; and $(x_n)$ converges to $L$ in $K$ when for every $\varepsilon > 0$ in $K$ there is $N$ with $|x_n - L| < \varepsilon$ for all $n \ge N$ ([[def-sequences-in-an-ordered-field]]).
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-259-
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-260-[L5] Every nonempty subset of $\mathbb{N}$ has a least element ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]).
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-261-
research/audit/wave6-certifier-tasks/thm-laurent-cauchy-complete.md-262-[L6] The order on $\mathbb{N}$ is total ([[thm-nat-linear-order]], [[def-nat-order]]), induction is available ([[thm-induction-principle]], [[def-natural-numbers]]), and every integer $\ge 0$ is the image of a unique natural number, so a natural number may be used as an index in $\mathbb{Z}$ ([[lem-nat-embeds-int]]).
--
