research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-824-## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-825-
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md:826:**Given:** The canonical naturals $\iota(n) = n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}}$; the sequence $x_k := 1/\iota(k+1)$; for a real $c > 0$ the sequence $y^{(c)}_k := c\,\iota(k+1)$; and the sequence $z_k := \iota(k+1)\,\iota(k+1)$.
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-827-
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-828-[L1] Canonical naturals: $\iota(n) > 0$ and invertible for $n \ge 1$, $\iota$ is strictly increasing, and $\iota(n) \ge 1$ for $n \ge 1$ ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]], [[def-nat-order]], [[thm-nat-linear-order]]).
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-829-
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-830-[L2] Archimedean facts: for every real $\eta > 0$ there is a natural $p \ge 1$ with $1/p < \eta$, and for every real $M$ there is a natural $p \ge 1$ with $M < \iota(p)$; and $0 < u < v$ gives $0 < 1/v < 1/u$ ([[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]], [[thm-of-archimedean]], [[lem-of-inverse-positive]]).
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-831-
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-832-[L3] Convergence to a real and divergence to $+\infty$; to establish convergence it suffices to produce a threshold for every real $\varepsilon > 0$; a constant sequence converges to its value ([[def-real-limit]], [[def-sequence]], [[def-divergence-to-infinity]]).
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-833-
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-834-[L4] A sequence diverging to $+\infty$ is unbounded and therefore does not converge to any real ([[def-divergence-to-infinity]], [[lem-convergent-implies-bounded]]); a limit, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-limit-unique]]).
--
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1030-  that is a strictly stronger condition ([[fs-sup-belongs-to-set]]).
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1031-- Bounds come in half-lines: if $\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ then so is every
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1032-  $\ell'' \le \ell$, and if $u$ is an upper bound then so is every $u'' \ge u$.
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1033-  Consequently a set that has one bound of a given kind has infinitely many, and
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1034-  the interesting question is whether the collection of them has a best element,
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1035-  which is what a supremum ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) or an infimum
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1036-  ([[def-infimum]]) is.
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1037-- Bounded above and bounded below are independent conditions. The set
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md:1038:  $A = \{\, n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}} : n \ge 1 \,\}$ of canonical naturals of
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1039-  $\mathbb{R}$ is bounded below by $0$ ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]]) and is not
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1040-  bounded above ([[thm-of-archimedean]]); its reflection $-A$ is bounded above
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1041-  and not bounded below ([[lem-reflection]]).
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1042-- The empty set is bounded, and vacuously so: every real number is both an upper
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1043-  bound and a lower bound of $\emptyset$, since the defining condition quantifies
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1044-  over no elements. Having bounds is therefore much weaker than having a least
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/thm-limsup-submultiplicative.md-1045-  upper bound or a greatest lower bound ([[fs-every-set-has-sup]]).
